1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,243
Everything on Linux is a file or a directory,

2
00:00:03,263 --> 00:00:08,206
and nearly every task starts with creating, copying, moving, or deleting them.

3
00:00:08,226 --> 00:00:11,388
A handful of short commands does almost all of it.

4
00:00:11,858 --> 00:00:16,169
In this video: create, delete, copy, and move files and directories.

5
00:00:20,189 --> 00:00:22,772
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,242 --> 00:00:26,614
Create, delete, copy, and move files and directories.

7
00:00:27,084 --> 00:00:31,663
We'll build a small project folder, copy things around, rename and move them,

8
00:00:31,683 --> 00:00:33,336
and then delete them safely.

9
00:00:42,856 --> 00:00:47,721
mkdir, make directory, creates a directory. We make one called projects.

10
00:00:51,191 --> 00:00:56,021
Paths work from where you are. projects/docs puts docs inside projects.

11
00:00:56,041 --> 00:00:58,958
This works because projects already exists.

12
00:01:02,428 --> 00:01:08,556
-p means parents. It creates the whole chain at once, here src and app inside it.

13
00:01:08,576 --> 00:01:12,883
Without -p it would fail, because src does not exist yet.

14
00:01:16,353 --> 00:01:22,720
ls -R lists a directory and everything inside it, level by level. Our tree:

15
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:26,807
docs, src, and app inside src. All empty so far.

16
00:01:33,327 --> 00:01:37,787
touch, from the last video, makes an empty file. a.txt goes in docs.

17
00:01:41,257 --> 00:01:45,230
echo with the greater-than sign makes a file with text in it.

18
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:46,997
b.txt holds the word hello.

19
00:01:50,467 --> 00:01:52,943
ls on the directory shows both files.

20
00:01:57,463 --> 00:02:03,186
cp copies. The pattern is always the same: command, source, destination.

21
00:02:03,206 --> 00:02:08,451
First we copy b.txt to a new name, b-copy.txt, in the same folder.

22
00:02:13,921 --> 00:02:17,169
If the destination is an existing directory,

23
00:02:17,189 --> 00:02:22,072
the copy keeps its name and goes inside. Here b.txt goes into src.

24
00:02:25,542 --> 00:02:31,074
A directory needs -r, for recursive. It copies the directory and everything in it.

25
00:02:31,094 --> 00:02:36,018
We copy docs to backup. Since backup does not exist, it becomes the copy.

26
00:02:40,488 --> 00:02:43,868
ls backup shows all three files came along.

27
00:02:43,888 --> 00:02:47,742
And ls projects/src shows app, and the copy of b.

28
00:02:54,262 --> 00:02:59,124
mv moves, and the pattern is the same: source, then destination.

29
00:02:59,144 --> 00:03:04,845
If both are in the same folder, it simply renames. a.txt becomes notes.txt.

30
00:03:09,315 --> 00:03:14,213
If the destination is a directory, the file moves inside it.

31
00:03:14,233 --> 00:03:16,591
b-copy.txt goes into src/app.

32
00:03:20,061 --> 00:03:25,050
ls shows the results. docs has b and notes. app has b-copy.

33
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:28,446
Unlike cp, mv leaves no original behind.

34
00:03:33,966 --> 00:03:39,429
rm removes a file. There is no trash can: it is gone. We remove notes.txt.

35
00:03:42,899 --> 00:03:46,507
rm refuses a directory: it says is a directory.

36
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:52,607
rmdir removes directories, but only empty ones, so it says directory not empty.

37
00:03:57,077 --> 00:04:01,082
rm -r removes a directory and everything inside it.

38
00:04:01,102 --> 00:04:06,529
Use it carefully, and check the path first. Now src holds just b.txt.

39
00:04:13,049 --> 00:04:16,485
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

40
00:04:16,955 --> 00:04:21,479
Files on disk survive a reboot. We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

41
00:04:24,949 --> 00:04:27,807
Uptime says zero minutes, so this is a fresh boot.

42
00:04:27,827 --> 00:04:31,606
Our lab eight files are still here, and the checker confirms them.

43
00:04:37,126 --> 00:04:42,666
Your turn. In your home directory, create lab8 with two subdirectories, a and b.

44
00:04:42,686 --> 00:04:46,002
Put a file one.txt holding the word data into a.

45
00:04:48,972 --> 00:04:52,343
Copy it into b. Then rename the one in a to two.txt.

46
00:04:52,363 --> 00:04:55,864
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

47
00:05:03,384 --> 00:05:05,583
Next up: Create hard and soft links.

48
00:05:06,053 --> 00:05:08,955
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
